From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 03:52:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(task): add a runner that drives plan, policy, checks and
 report

Seventh slice, and the one that puts the earlier pieces to work: TaskRunner is
the first code that actually calls RiskEngine, TaskStateMachine, TaskResume,
FailureClassifier and TaskReportBuilder in one flow.

- The order is the one the task document asks for: plan -> assess every step
  with the policy engine -> ask once (worst level + all reasons) when anything
  needs approval -> execute with retries bounded by TaskLimits -> verify with the
  declared checks -> report.
- A DENY stops the task before anything runs and is reported as
  REFUSED_BY_POLICY; declining the approval cancels it with nothing executed. A
  permissive PolicyConfig still cannot auto-approve a denial.
- Resuming goes through TaskResume: an interrupted side-effecting step is asked
  about ("it may already have taken effect") and never silently repeated, the
  attempt budget is spent, and the wall clock is not reset. A terminal task is
  refused with a stated reason.
- Limits are enforced here, not by the caller: attempts per step, wall clock
  checked before each step, and every step assessment is written to the audit
  log with its actor.
- TaskPorts (approve / execute / runCheck) is the whole outside world, so the
  orchestration is unit tested with fakes and core stays free of android.*.

Errors found by running the tests, and fixed: an empty plan reached verification
and came back VERIFIED; a retry budget that ran out was reported as a plain
failure instead of a retry exhaustion; and the environment limitation pasted the
raw log line instead of saying the check never ran.

Tests: TaskRunnerTest (18).
