+# Task Lifecycle, Runner and Report
+
+This is the piece that ties the core together: `TaskRunner` drives one task from request to report, using
+the policy engine, the state machine, the resume rules and the check classifier that already existed.
+
+## Files
+
+| File | Responsibility |
+| --- | --- |
+| `core/task/TaskRunner.kt` | `TaskRunner`, `TaskPorts`, `PlannedStep`, `TaskRequest`, `TaskRun`, `StepOutcome`, `ApprovalRequest` |
+| `core/task/TaskReport.kt` | `StepRecord`, `TaskVerdict`, `ReportCaveat`, `TaskReport`, `TaskReportInput`, `TaskReportBuilder` |
+| `core/task/TaskState.kt`, `TaskStateMachine.kt` | States and the transitions allowed between them (unchanged) |
+| `core/task/TaskProgress.kt`, `TaskLimits.kt`, `TaskResume.kt` | Saved progress, hard stops, resume/confirm/cancel rules (unchanged) |
+
+Tests: `TaskRunnerTest` (18), `TaskReportBuilderTest` (13), `TaskStateMachineTest` (11), `TaskResumeTest` (13).
+
+## The order the runner follows
+
+```
+DRAFT → INSPECTING → PLANNED
+   assess every step with RiskEngine
+     ├─ any DENY   → FAILED, nothing runs, report says REFUSED_BY_POLICY
+     └─ any REQUIRE_APPROVAL → AWAITING_APPROVAL → ask once (worst level + all reasons)
+                                └─ declined → CANCELLED, nothing runs
+AWAITING_APPROVAL|PLANNED → READY → RUNNING
+   per step: execute, retry only if the step says the failure is retryable,
+             stop on the first hard failure, wall clock checked before each step
+RUNNING → VERIFYING
+   run the declared checks
+     ├─ all green → CHECKS_PASSED
+     └─ otherwise → FAILED
+→ TaskReport with a verdict
+```
+
+Resuming (`run(request, previous = savedProgress)`) goes through `TaskResume` first: an interrupted
+side-effecting step is asked about ("it may already have taken effect"), never silently repeated, the
+attempt budget is spent, and the wall clock is *not* reset. A terminal task is refused with a stated reason.
+
+## Ports, and why
+
+`TaskPorts` is three functions: `approve`, `execute`, `runCheck`. That is the whole outside world the runner
+needs, which is why every rule above is unit tested with fakes and no emulator. The Android implementation
+plugs in here:
+
+- `approve` → the existing approval UI (`PermissionRequest` sheet), showing `ApprovalRequest.reasons`;
+- `execute` → the runtime's tool call for that step, via `ToolCallMapper` on the way in;
+- `runCheck` → the existing terminal/runtime process runner, with `CheckCommand.timeoutMs` /
+  `maxOutputBytes` applied, feeding `FailureClassifier.result(...)`.
+
+Writing this as an interface is also what keeps `core` free of `android.*`, so the whole package still
+compiles and runs on a plain JVM.
+
+## The verdict, and why it is separate from the state
+
+`TaskState.CHECKS_PASSED` says "the machine got here". `TaskVerdict` says "and here is what that proves":
+
+| Verdict | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| `VERIFIED` | finished, and every required check actually ran and passed |
+| `NOT_VERIFIED` | finished-looking, but no checks ran, a check could not run, or the task is not done |
+| `FAILED` | a step or a check failed |
+| `CANCELLED` | the user declined, or the policy engine refused the plan |
+
+The builder is deliberately pessimistic: any failed step, any failed check, an empty check list, an
+environment-blocked check, or a state short of the success states all block `VERIFIED`, and each adds a
+`ReportCaveat` the report renders in a "not verified" section. An empty plan cannot reach verification at
+all.
+
+`TaskReport` carries request, steps (with per-step attempts and failure), changed paths, checks with their
+classification and one-line evidence, the policy decisions from the audit log, recorded memory fact ids,
+limitations, `needsNewApk`, and the duration. `render()` is a convenience text view for a PR body or a bug
+report; the data is the source of truth.
+
+## What is still missing
+
+- **Persistence.** `TaskProgress` is plumbed through `run(previous = ...)`, but nothing writes it to disk yet.
+- **The UI.** No screen calls `TaskRunner` yet; the task centre, plan sheet and report view are the next
+  change, together with the settings that produce `PolicyConfig`.
+- **Real ports.** The three functions above have no Android implementation in this change, which is why the
+  runner is described here as tested, not as in use.
+- **Per-test parsing.** `CheckResult` classifies a failure; it does not list which tests failed.
